 "reviewHtml": "<div><p>Rather than flit from one theme to another as seasons pass, over Labrum’s past three shows, Foday Dumbuya has ruminated on the topic of osmosis. Not the physical phenomenon, mind you, rather the process taken as an allegory for the history of intercultural cross-pollination and how the movement of peoples has colored the weave of Britain’s contemporary cultural fabric.</p><p>Where the two previous collections adopted more specific thematic frameworks for their excavations of the topic—first music, then textile production—today’s offering was ostensibly a more holistic proposal: “It’s the final part of a trilogy,” Dumbaya said in a preview, implying a sense of narrative completion, of rounding out.</p><p>Those who came seeking a satisfying conclusion, however, will have been left wanting a little. The fact that it was presented beneath the same painted ceilings and genteel portraits of the Edwardian hall Dumbuya took to last season—fielding a collection widely regarded as his best to date, a consensus vindicated by his winning of the BFC/<em>GQ</em> Designer Fashion Fund shortly after—naturally meant that whatever bowed here would be subject to direct comparison.</p><p>While that previous collection felt like a ramping up, a pivot toward a sharper, sparer, more severe design language, this offering seemed to coast on its momentum rather than propel itself further. Which isn’t to say there wasn’t much to like here. An earthy palette of burgundies, mossy greens, and ochres, punctuated by flashes of cobalt—lifted from Jacob Armstead Lawrence’s abstract figurative tableaux—exuded a lush warmth, heralding a return of color following fall 2026’s relatively spare, monochrome palette. Also, the textiles from which this season’s ample tailoring was cut—custom-developed ochre fil coupe, spongy bouclé the color of young hay—called out to be felt. The season’s primary embroidery story, which drew inspiration from the life of Dumbuya’s grandmother, a Sierra Leonean produce seller, was so richly vibrant and detailed that it prompted a double take to verify that the graphics were, in fact, rendered in thread.</p><p>This was a technically accomplished, craft-filled collection: The fusion of British and West African sartorial craft techniques has been Dumbuya’s MO for a good while now. What felt lacking, though, was the discursive punchiness and call-to-action urgency that has defined his recent collections. Then again, perhaps this was the right way to round out this extended contemplation of osmosis. The end point of the process (whether literal or figurative) is equilibrium; the jostle, excitement, and friction of movement lulling down into a still, subdued status quo.</p></div>",
